  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 AM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nDry weather will persist through the upcoming week as high\npressure continues to hold sway across the eastern half of the \nCONUS. Within the dry airmass, daytime temperatures will continue\nto trend above normal, with mid to upper 80s increasingly common \nthrough Friday. By this weekend, a shift to more easterly low\nlevel flow, resulting in a wedging effect around the \nAppalachians, will begin to cut in to daytime highs somewhat. \nMorning lows will remain seasonably cool through the next several \ndays.\n\nFire weather will remain the primarily concern over the upcoming\nweek, given antecedent dry ground conditions/fuels (daily max \nERCs in the 90th+ percentile) with RHs falling below 30 percent \neach afternoon and at times falling into the teens. The saving \ngrace preventing a more critical situation worthy of red flag \nproducts will be relatively light wind each day, which should help\ncontrol spread of fires that do develop. Nevertheless, caution \nshould continue to be exercised with any outdoor burning over the \nnext several days. Heed local burn bans, which continue to be \nadded in new areas. If we begin to see signs in later forecasts \nof stronger wind on any upcoming days, messaging may need to be \nelevated accordingly. /DL/",
